{:edb/db-before-t 1679 :edb/db-after-t 1680 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534992 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:37:34.904Z" 13194139534992 true] [17592186166625 1145 "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f$. That is, there exists a function $f$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\nabla f\n$$\n\nThe function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf F$.\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f$, we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f &= \\frac{∂f}{∂x}i + \\frac{∂f}{∂y}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sin (2x - y))i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sin (2x - y))j \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (2x - y)i - \\cos (2x - y)j \\\\\n&= F\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = 2\\cos(2x-y)\\,\\mathbf i -\\cos(2x-y) \\, \\mathbf j\n$$" 13194139534992 false] [17592186166625 1145 "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = 2\\cos(2x-y)\\,\\mathbf i -\\cos(2x-y) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186166648 1100 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf F = (e^{y^2} - 2xy) \\, \\mathbf i + (2xye^{y^2} - y) \\,\\mathbf j$\n2. $\\mathbf G = (3x^2 - y^2) \\, \\mathbf i + (4 - 2xy) \\,\\mathbf j$\n3. $\\mathbf H = (xy^2 + 1) \\, \\mathbf i + 2xy \\,\\mathbf j$" 13194139534992 false] [17592186166648 1100 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf F = (e^{y^2} - 2xy) \\, \\mathbf i + (2xye^{y^2} - y) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf G = (3x^2 - y^2) \\, \\mathbf i + (4 - 2xy) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf H = (xy^2 + 1) \\, \\mathbf i + 2xy \\,\\mathbf j$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186166648 1145 "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nbe a vector field with domain\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D$. Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n> $\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D$.*\n\nNote that for each of our functions,\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F$, we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}$.\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G$, we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y$.\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$, we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H$, we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y$.\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534992 false] [17592186166648 1145 "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534992 true] [17592186166649 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a$." 13194139534992 false] [17592186166649 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186166649 1145 "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nbe a vector field with domain\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D$. Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n> $\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D$.*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F$, the domain\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2$. Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2a}{1 + 9x^{2}} &= \\frac{3}{1 + 9x^{2}} \\\\\n2a &= 3 \\\\\na &= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139534992 false] [17592186166649 1145 "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ the domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a}{1+9{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{1+9{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186166775 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325373 13194139534992 true] [17592186166775 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325380 13194139534992 true] [17592186166775 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325387 13194139534992 true] [17592186166795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325408 13194139534992 true] [17592186166795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325415 13194139534992 true] [17592186166795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325429 13194139534992 true] [17592186166796 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325394 13194139534992 true] [17592186166796 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325401 13194139534992 true] [17592186166796 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325422 13194139534992 true] [17592186166796 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325436 13194139534992 true] [17592186166981 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534992 true] [17592186166981 1100 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x, y) = ye^{xy}$ a potential function?" 13194139534992 false] [17592186166981 1100 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ a potential function?" 13194139534992 true] [17592186166981 1145 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j.\n$$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186166983 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534992 true] [17592186166983 1100 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $F = xe^{xy}i + ye^{xy}j$\n2. $G = (xy + y^{2})i + (xy + x^{2})j$\n3. $H = \\cos (x - y)i - \\cos (x - y)j$" 13194139534992 false] [17592186166983 1100 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=x{e}^{xy}\\,i+y{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- $G=(xy+{y}^{2})\\,i+(xy+{x}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,j$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186166983 1145 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=y{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=xy+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=xy+{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)$ and $Q=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534992 true] [17592186167028 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534992 true] [17592186167028 1100 "Consider the vector field $F = 2ayi + (3 - x)j$ on $R^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a$." 13194139534992 false] [17592186167028 1100 "Consider the vector field $F=2ay\\,i+(3-x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186167028 1145 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=2ay,\\,Q=3-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & =-1 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186167257 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534992 true] [17592186167257 1100 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $F = (x^{2} + 2y)i + (x^{2} + 2y)j$\n2. $G = (ye^{x} + 2)i + (e^{x} + 4y)j$\n3. $H = yi + xj$" 13194139534992 false] [17592186167257 1100 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=({x}^{2}+2y)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,j$\n- $G=(y{e}^{x}+2)\\,i+({e}^{x}+4y)\\,j$\n- $H=y\\,i+x\\,j$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186167257 1145 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q={x}^{2}+2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y{e}^{x}+2$ and $Q={e}^{x}+4y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y$ and $Q=x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534992 true] [17592186167473 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534992 true] [17592186167473 1100 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x, y, z) = x^{2}yz + z^{2}$ a potential function?" 13194139534992 false] [17592186167473 1100 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2}$ a potential function?" 13194139534992 true] [17592186167473 1145 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k.\n$$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186325373 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325374 13194139534992 true] [17592186325373 1089 "q-104396" 13194139534992 true] [17592186325373 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534992 true] [17592186325373 1100 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$ a potential function?" 13194139534992 true] [17592186325373 1113 #uuid "e26fb992-29b1-59c4-9818-c4b0755162ec" 13194139534992 true] [17592186325373 1145 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2x\\,i-j.\n$$" 13194139534992 true] [17592186325374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325375 13194139534992 true] [17592186325374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325376 13194139534992 true] [17592186325374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325377 13194139534992 true] [17592186325374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325378 13194139534992 true] [17592186325374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325379 13194139534992 true] [17592186325374 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534992 true] [17592186325374 1041 "selection" 13194139534992 true] [17592186325374 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325375 13194139534992 true] [17592186325374 1110 #uuid "8c1fcb6f-a289-5971-a0f7-8103c42451fb" 13194139534992 true] [17592186325375 1034 "2x\\,i-j" 13194139534992 true] [17592186325375 1103 #edb/ref 17